// A function that can stop in the middle, and the three ways one is spelled.
//
// There is one machine underneath. `gen`, `async` and `async gen` differ only in which of its three
// slots are inhabited, and `never` in a slot removes the arm that carried it — so a generator's
// `step` has no `Waiting` case at all, rather than one that cannot happen.

import { Ticket, TicketBase } from "core/ticket.wac";

/**
 * What one step of a machine answered.
 *
 * A slot typed `never` removes its arm: `Step<never, i32, void>` is `Yielded | Done`, and a match
 * over it is exhaustive with two arms rather than three-with-a-dead-one.
 *
 * **That sentence was the only thing anywhere that defines `never`**, and it is not a definition —
 * it is a consequence. The definition is one line and it is here now:
 *
 * > **`never` is the empty union.** An enum with no variants, so no value of it exists.
 *
 * Everything the pages say about it follows, and three of them stop being separate questions:
 *
 *   * *a slot typed `never` removes its arm* — the arm's payload type has no values, so no `Step`
 *     can be `Waiting`, and a `match` that omits it is exhaustive. The sentence above, derived.
 *   * *`union<never, E>` reduces to `E`* — `{} ∪ S = S`. `../TECHNICAL.md` has this as a separate
 *     transform the lowering needs; it is the same fact.
 *   * *`never` satisfies any type* — vacuously, since there is nothing to assign. Note that this one
 *     is **not** free under the lowering `../TECHNICAL.md` measured: `union<A, B>` becomes an enum
 *     of one-field variants and assignability is nominal membership, so `never` needs its own
 *     one-line clause in the checker rather than falling out of a subset rule.
 *
 * Written down 2026-09-05, from `@/packages/wacc/src/check.wac` — which reached it by asking what a
 * checker would have to do and finding that the answer had never been stated. `never` appears in two
 * files in code and nineteen times by grep, *never* being an ordinary English word, and none of the
 * two said what it is.
 *
 * `../QUESTIONS.md` has what it still leaves open — chiefly whether a *dead* arm stays legal, because
 * with `W` opaque a generic body needs three and at `Step<never, …>` two suffice, and if three are
 * refused there the body cannot be written.
 */
export enum Step<W, Y, R> {
  Waiting(W wanted),
  Yielded(Y value),
  Done(R result)
}

/**
 * The machine. `W` is what it can block on, `Y` what it can yield, `R` what it ends with.
 *
 * The `coroutine` operator lowers any of the three spellings to one of these, and applying it to
 * something already lowered is a compile error rather than a no-op — the operator is a change of
 * representation and there is nothing for a second one to do.
 *
 * **"One machine, three spellings" is not quite true and the difference is observable.**
 * `TECHNICAL.md`'s example shows `coroutine s.tick()` running *nothing* — `s.n` is still 0 — and a
 * shipped `async` call runs the body eagerly to its first `await`, measured: `n = 1` before any
 * drain. So obtaining the machine by calling and obtaining it by `coroutine` differ at the moment
 * of obtaining it. They arguably *should*, since a machine you step by hand is the case where you
 * choose when it starts — but then this sentence is wrong rather than incomplete.
 * `../QUESTIONS.md`.
 */
export struct Coroutine<W, Y, R> {
  /**
   * Run to the next boundary. Stepping a finished machine is a no-op that answers `Done` again.
   *
   * **A funcref, for the reason `ticket.wac` gives at length.** This was a bodyless method with
   * `Generator` and `AsyncGenerator` inheriting it, and dispatch in wac is static — so a `Generator`
   * held as a `Coroutine`, which is exactly what `TECHNICAL.md`'s example does, would have run this
   * empty body. The `coroutine` operator builds one of these with the right lambda in the slot, and
   * what kind of machine it is is what the lambda does.
   */
  fn<Step<W, Y, R>()> step;
}

/** `gen<Y> R f(…)`. Blocks on nothing, so it has no `Waiting`. */
export struct Generator<Y, R> : Coroutine<never, Y, R> { }

/**
 * `async gen<Y> R f(…)`. Yields, and can also block.
 *
 * Its `step` is the async-ness written out: it answers a ticket for the next `Yielded | Done`,
 * rather than answering a third kind of thing. Everything async in the language is *wrapped in a
 * ticket* and this is no exception.
 */
export struct AsyncGenerator<Y, R> : Coroutine<TicketBase, Y, R> {
  fn<Ticket<Step<never, Y, R>>()> nextStep;
}

/**
 * `async R f(…)`, which is an `AsyncGenerator` that never yields.
 *
 * There is no type here because there does not need to be one. An async function's *call* answers
 * `Ticket<R>`, which is the thing a caller wants; the machine underneath is reachable with the
 * `coroutine` operator for the rare code that drives one by hand.
 */
